# Gender-Neutral User References

All user-facing text must refer to users, operators, employees, customers, or system actors using gender-neutral language.

Never assume the gender of the person behind a user account.

Avoid gendered labels such as:

* "Vendedora"
* "Vendedor"
* "La encargada"
* "El encargado"
* "La usuaria"
* "El usuario" when referring to a specific person in a UI label
* "Atendida por"
* "Atendido por" when the phrase itself introduces gender

Prefer neutral labels based on the person's role, action, or relationship to the record.

Recommended examples:

* `Atendido por:`
* `Registrado por:`
* `Creado por:`
* `Modificado por:`
* `Asignado a:`
* `Responsable:`
* `Autorizado por:`
* `Capturado por:`
* `Procesado por:`
* `Solicitado por:`
* `Entregado por:`
* `Recibido por:`
* `Usuario responsable:`
* `Persona responsable:`

## Action Descriptions

When describing an action performed by a user, avoid sentences that require gendered nouns.

Avoid:

* "La vendedora realizó la venta."
* "El vendedor registró el pedido."
* "La encargada autorizó el pago."

Prefer:

* "Venta registrada por: [Name]"
* "Pedido registrado por: [Name]"
* "Autorizado por: [Name]"
* "[Name] registró la venta."
* "[Name] procesó el pedido."

When possible, prefer action-based phrasing over gendered role-based phrasing.

## Applies to All New UI

This rule applies to all newly created or modified:

* labels;
* tables;
* column names;
* cards;
* forms;
* tooltips;
* notifications;
* activity logs;
* audit trails;
* reports;
* dashboards;
* confirmation messages;
* error messages;
* empty states;
* exports;
* printable documents.

## Existing Text

When modifying an existing screen or component, if nearby user-facing text contains unnecessary gendered references, update those references to neutral wording when doing so is safe and within the scope of the change.

Do not change the meaning of business roles that are officially gender-specific by definition. Otherwise, default to neutral wording.

## Required Principle

**Refer to the action, responsibility, role, or account — never assume the person's gender.**

When multiple valid phrasings exist, prefer the shortest, clearest, and most natural gender-neutral wording for the interface.
